      },
      "output": "**Assumption:** You are a mechanical\u2011engineering student preparing for a thermodynamics final on the 20th and have roughly 2\u202fh\u202f/\u202fday to study.\n\n**Two\u2011week study schedule (Day\u202f1\u202f\u2013\u202fDay\u202f14)**  \n\n| Day | Focus | Action (\u22482\u202fh) |\n|-----|-------|----------------|\n| 1 | Foundations | Review lecture notes on the First Law; write a one\u2011page summary in your own words. |\n| 2 | Foundations | Solve 5 problems on closed\u2011system energy balances (textbook\u202f\u00a73.1). |\n| 3 | Properties of pure substances | Watch a 15\u2011min video on P\u2011v\u2011T diagrams; sketch the three phases for water. |\n| 4 | Properties of pure substances | Complete 4 textbook exercises on saturated\u2011steam tables. |\n| 5 | Second Law \u2013 concepts | Read the textbook section on entropy; create a 5\u2011bullet \u201centropy checklist\u201d (state, process, sign). |\n| 6 | Second Law \u2013 applications | Work through 3 problems on entropy change of ideal gases. |\n| 7 | Review & integration | Combine your summaries; redraw all key diagrams from memory. |\n| 8 | Power cycles | Study the Rankine cycle layout; list each component\u2019s role. |\n| 9 | Power cycles | Solve a complete Rankine\u2011cycle problem (heat input, work output, efficiency). |\n|10 | Refrigeration & heat pumps | Summarize the refrigeration cycle steps; compare COP formulas. |\n|11 | Refrigeration & heat pumps | Practice 3 COP problems (one for refrigerator, one for heat pump, one for air\u2011conditioner). |\n|12 | Mixed review | Do a timed 20\u2011question mixed\u2011topic quiz (use past exam or online bank). |\n|13 | Weak\u2011spot focus | Identify 2\u20133 questions you missed; re\u2011work those problems with full solutions. |\n|14 | Final polish | Write a concise \u201ccheat sheet\u201d (one side of A4) with key equations, sign conventions, and typical values; test yourself by covering the sheet and recalling each item. |\n\n**Next immediate step:** Grab your thermodynamics textbook, open the section on the First Law, and write the one\u2011page summary today (Day\u202f1).  \n\n**Check:** After finishing the summary, can you state in a sentence why the internal energy change of a closed system equals heat added minus work done by the system?",
