      "review": {
        "passed": true,
        "words": 413,
        "violations": []
      },
      "output": "**Reading\u2011and\u2011analysis plan (14\u202fdays)**  \n\n| Day | Activity |\n|-----|----------|\n| 1   | List the core research question(s) and any sub\u2011questions; write a one\u2011sentence statement of the expected contribution. |\n| 2   | Identify three \u201cmust\u2011read\u201d seminal works in urban economics that directly address your topic (e.g., classic theory, major empirical study, key methodological text). Obtain them (library, PDF, inter\u2011library loan). |\n| 3   | Skim each seminal work: note abstract, introduction, and conclusion; write a 2\u2011sentence summary of its relevance to your question. |\n| 4   | Compile a bibliography of recent (last\u202f5\u202fyears) journal articles that cite the seminal works; use Google Scholar \u201ccited by\u201d lists or EconLit. Export citations to a reference manager. |\n| 5   | Read the abstracts of the recent articles; flag those that (a) use a similar data set, (b) apply a comparable empirical strategy, or (c) challenge the seminal findings. |\n| 6   | Select the five most relevant recent articles; read each in depth (intro, methods, results, discussion). Write a 150\u2011word critical note for each, focusing on gaps you could fill. |\n| 7   | Map the literature: create a simple table (author, year, data, method, key finding, identified gap). |\n| 8   | Review methodological literature on the primary econometric technique you plan to use (e.g., spatial regression, difference\u2011in\u2011differences). Gather two methodological guides or textbook chapters. |\n| 9   | Summarize each methodological source (key assumptions, strengths, limitations) in a 100\u2011word memo; note which assumptions your data will satisfy. |\n| 10  | Identify data sources needed for your analysis (census, administrative, GIS, etc.). Locate the official repositories or data portals; note access requirements and download procedures. |\n| 11  | Draft a data\u2011collection checklist: variables, time periods, geographic units, cleaning steps. |\n| 12  | Outline the analytical framework: hypothesis, model specification, identification strategy. Write a one\u2011page schematic linking literature gaps to your proposed contribution. |\n| 13  | Write a brief \u201cliterature\u2011to\u2011method\u201d narrative (\u2248300\u202fwords) that justifies your chosen approach based on the gaps identified. |\n| 14  | Consolidate all notes, bibliography, and the schematic into a single \u201cliterature review draft\u201d folder; back up the files. |\n\n*Assumption:* Your dissertation will involve an empirical urban\u2011economics analysis using standard econometric tools and publicly available data. If your topic is purely theoretical or uses a different methodology, the focus of days\u202f8\u201112 would need adjustment.\n\n**Next step:**\u202fWhich specific research question or hypothesis are you planning to test in your dissertation?",
