      "output": "**Pick:** iPhone SE (3rd\u202fgen, 2022) \u2013 typically \u00a3429\u2011\u00a3499 new.  \n**Runner\u2011up:** iPhone\u202f13\u202fmini (used or refurbished) \u2013 typically \u00a3549\u2011\u00a3649.\n\n**Why this works**  \n1. **Ecosystem fit** \u2013 both run iOS, sync seamlessly with your iPhone and Apple Watch.  \n2. **Longevity** \u2013 Apple supports devices for ~5\u20116\u202fyears; the SE\u2019s A15 chip matches the iPhone\u202f13 line, so software updates will last well beyond your 4\u2011year keep\u2011time.  \n3. **Budget** \u2013 the SE sits comfortably under your \u00a3700 ceiling; the 13\u202fmini, if you can find a good\u2011condition refurbished unit, stays within it while giving a larger display and better cameras.  \n\n**Key trade\u2011off** \u2013 The SE saves money and offers the same processor as the 13\u202fmini but has a smaller 4.7\u2011in screen and a single\u2011lens rear camera; the 13\u202fmini costs more but gives a 5.4\u2011in OLED display and dual\u2011lens photography, which matters only if you need higher\u2011quality photos or a larger screen for media.  \n\n**Next steps**  \n1. Check current pricing for a new iPhone\u202fSE (3rd\u202fgen) and for reputable refurbished iPhone\u202f13\u202fmini sellers.  \n2. Compare the screen size and camera specs against how you actually use the phone (e.g., heavy video/photography vs. basic calls, messaging, apps).  \n3. Decide if the extra camera capability and OLED screen justify the modest price premium.\n\n**One quick question:** How important is a larger, higher\u2011quality display and dual\u2011camera system to you versus saving the extra \u00a3150\u2011\u00a3200?",
