      "output": "**Reading\u2011and\u2011Analysis Plan (14\u202fdays)**  \n\n*Assumption: you are a PhD candidate in urban economics and need a focused schedule to gather core literature, map the debate, and start a structured analysis for your dissertation.*\n\n| Day | Activity | Output |\n|-----|----------|--------|\n| 1\u20112 | Identify\u202f5\u202fkey \u201cmust\u2011read\u201d papers (classic theory, latest empirical work, and one methodological guide). Use your university library\u2019s citation\u2011search and the reference lists of recent review articles. | List of citations (full reference, PDF link). |\n| 3\u20114 | Skim each paper: abstract, intro, conclusion, and figures/tables. Note the central research question, data sources, and main findings in a 1\u2011paragraph summary. | Summary table (paper\u202f\u2192\u202fquestion\u202f/\u202fdata\u202f/\u202ffinding). |\n| 5\u20116 | Deep\u2011read the two most relevant empirical studies. Highlight assumptions, model specifications, and robustness checks. Record any gaps or contradictory results. | Annotated PDFs + gap\u2011list (bullet points). |\n| 7 | Read the methodological guide (e.g., spatial econometrics, causal inference). Write a 200\u2011word note on which techniques could suit your own data. | Method note. |\n| 8\u20119 | Map the literature: create a simple concept map (research question \u2192 theoretical strands \u2192 empirical approaches \u2192 data). Use a free tool (e.g., draw.io) and keep it editable. | Concept map file. |\n| 10\u201111 | Draft a \u201cliterature synthesis\u201d outline: sections, key arguments, and where each paper will be cited. Include a paragraph on identified gaps that your dissertation will address. | Outline document. |\n| 12 | Choose your analytical framework (e.g., difference\u2011in\u2011differences, hedonic pricing, GIS\u2011based spatial regression). Write a brief justification linking it to the gaps you noted. | Framework justification (\u2248150\u202fwords). |\n| 13\u201114 | Compile a \u201cto\u2011do\u201d list for data acquisition and preliminary coding, referencing the methods you selected. Set milestones (e.g., data download by week\u202f2, cleaning by week\u202f3). | Action checklist with dates you will assign. |\n\n**Next step you can start today:** locate the three most\u2011cited recent review articles in urban economics (search terms: \u201curban economics review 2020\u20112023\u201d) and add them to your Day\u202f1\u20112 list.  \n\n*Is there a specific sub\u2011topic or geographic focus you want the plan to prioritize?*",
