      "output": "**Recommendation:** Enroll in a focused, project\u2011based data\u2011science bootcamp (or part\u2011time master\u2011level specialization) while you continue your junior analyst role, then leverage the new portfolio to apply for data\u2011scientist positions within Berlin.\n\n**Trade\u2011off:** This path costs time (\u22486\u20119\u202fmonths of part\u2011time study) and tuition, but it\u2019s reversible and keeps your income stable; a pure job\u2011switch now would likely land you another analyst role rather than a scientist role given your current experience.\n\n**Next actions**\n1. Identify 2\u20113 reputable, part\u2011time programs in Berlin or online (e.g.,\u202fDataCamp, Springboard, or a local university\u2019s \u201cData Science for Professionals\u201d track) that include a capstone project and career support; shortlist by\u202fearly\u202fnext week.  \n2. Allocate 10\u202fhours per week to a \u201cportfolio project\u201d that uses real\u2011world data (e.g.,\u202fpublic Berlin transport datasets) to showcase end\u2011to\u2011end modeling, feature engineering, and deployment; set a 4\u2011week sprint plan and start this week.  \n3. Once you\u2019ve selected a program, apply for any available scholarships or employer tuition\u2011reimbursement (ask your manager for a learning\u2011budget discussion) and commit to the first module within the next month.  \n\n*Is there a particular area of data science (e.g.,\u202fNLP, computer vision, forecasting) you\u2019re most interested in pursuing?*",
