      "output": "**Reading\u2011and\u2011analysis plan (14\u202fdays)**  \n\n| Day | Activity |\n|-----|----------|\n| 1   | List 5\u20137 core keywords (theoretical framework, geographic focus, key variables). |\n| 2   | Search academic databases (Google\u202fScholar, Scopus, EconLit) using Boolean combos of the keywords; export citations to a reference manager. |\n| 3   | Scan titles/abstracts; keep the 20 most relevant papers; note their research questions and data sources. |\n| 4   | Retrieve full texts of the 20 papers; create a spreadsheet with columns: citation, methodology, main finding, data used, gaps identified. |\n| 5   | Read each paper\u2019s introduction and conclusion; fill \u201cresearch question\u201d and \u201cgap\u201d columns. |\n| 6   | Group the papers by methodological approach (e.g., structural model, difference\u2011in\u2011differences, spatial econometrics). |\n| 7   | For each group, read the methods section in depth; summarize assumptions, identification strategy, and robustness checks. |\n| 8   | Identify the most frequently used data sets (e.g., ACS, CENSUS, panel of city\u2011level indicators). |\n| 9   | Locate the original data sources; download documentation and a sample of the data. |\n| 10  | Replicate a key descriptive table from one seminal paper to verify data handling steps. |\n| 11  | Draft a comparative matrix of methodological strengths/weaknesses relative to your dissertation aim. |\n| 12  | Write a 300\u2011word synthesis of the literature gaps that your work will address. |\n| 13  | Outline the analytical steps you will follow (data cleaning, model specification, validation). |\n| 14  | Review the whole plan; adjust the reading list or methods matrix as needed; set next\u2011week milestones. |\n\n*Assumption:* you have roughly two weeks to build a solid literature foundation and a preliminary methods blueprint before deeper empirical work begins.  \n\n**Next step for me:**\u202fWhat is the specific dissertation topic (e