      "output": "**Answer**  \nTo hit a flat\u2011deposit in three years you need a concrete monthly savings target. In Dublin a typical first\u2011time buyer aims for a 10\u202f%\u201120\u202f% deposit on the expected purchase price. Assuming you\u2019re looking at a \u20ac350\u202fk property (a common mid\u2011range figure), the deposit range is \u20ac35\u202fk\u2013\u20ac70\u202fk. Split over 36\u202fmonths that means saving **\u20ac970\u2013\u20ac1\u202f950 per month**.\n\n**Why the range matters**  \n- **Lower end (10\u202f%)** keeps the monthly outflow modest but may limit the properties you can consider or force a longer mortgage term.  \n- **Higher end (20\u202f%)** reduces the loan\u2011to\u2011value ratio, giving you better mortgage rates and more negotiating power, but requires a tighter cash\u2011flow discipline.\n\n**Downside test**  \nIf you lose a month\u2019s income or face an unexpected \u20ac2\u202fk expense, the plan should still survive. Build a buffer of at least one month\u2019s target savings in an easy\u2011access emergency fund before you start the 36\u2011month run\u2011rate. That way a bad month only delays the target by one month rather than derailing it.\n\n**Next actions you can take this week**  \n1. **Set a deposit goal** \u2013 decide whether you\u2019re comfortable aiming for 10\u202f% or 20\u202f% of your target property price. Write the \u20ac amount down.  \n2. **Create a cash\u2011flow snapshot** \u2013 list net monthly income and all fixed outgoings (rent, utilities, transport, subscriptions). Subtract to see how much discretionary cash you have.  \n3. **Open a dedicated high\u2011interest savings account** (or an existing one you can earmark) and automate a transfer of the chosen monthly amount on payday.  \n\n*Question:* Which deposit percentage (10\u202f% or 20\u202f%) feels realistic given your current cash\u2011flow snapshot?",
